decisions:
  - id: TD-video-project-split
    decision: VideoProject types live in @a-company/atelier-types (zero-runtime); filesystem utilities live in the CLI package (Node.js fs scope)
    rationale: 'Honors the existing architectural split — types package has no runtime, video package is browser-only (DOM lib), CLI is the Node-side surface. Cross-package import works since CLI already devDepends on atelier-types.'
  - id: TD-idempotent-scaffold
    decision: createVideoProject does not overwrite existing files in the target folder
    rationale: 'Re-running atelier edit on an existing project should never destroy in-progress work. existsSync guards on source copy, composition, cuts.json mean a second invocation is a no-op for any file already present.'
  - id: TD-draft-composition-defers-metadata
    decision: createVideoProject writes a draft composition with placeholder 1920x1080 canvas and no videoMeta — atelier edit (T-005) fills in real values after probing the source
    rationale: 'createVideoProject is filesystem-only by design — no ffprobe dependency at this layer. T-005 owns the metadata probe and updates the composition + asset.videoMeta before opening in studio.'
learnings:
  - 'CLI tsc --noEmit covers test files too — strict noUnusedLocals will catch dead-code drift in tests, not just src. Worth keeping; caught a real meaningless assertion (expect(true).toBe(true)) in canvas/index.test.ts.'
  - 'pnpm -r reports "Scope: 14 of 15 workspace projects" — the 15th is the repo root (no test script, which is correct). Not a missing test target.'
  - 'CLI declares @a-company/atelier-studio: ">=0.25.1" as a registry-version runtime dependency, not workspace:*. This is a deliberate publishing strategy — at npm install time, users pull the published studio rather than relying on bundled code. Worth knowing during coordinated version bumps; not a bug.'
  - 'A package with skeleton-only providers (no tests) makes pnpm -r test exit non-zero across the workspace. Always add at least a smoke test alongside a skeleton — verifies imports, option defaults, and pre-implementation throw behavior.'
